# Bot Architecture Patterns

## Command structure

Each command file registers handlers and exports nothing. Side effects only.

```
commands/
  start.ts       — onboarding, upsert user
  menu.ts        — main inline menu + all action handlers
  createWarranty.ts  — admin /w
  newBroadcast.ts    — admin /nb
  broadcasts.ts      — admin /b
  users.ts           — admin /u
  batteryRequest.ts  — battery selection (Max) / redirect (TG)
  message.ts         — state machine for multi-step flows
  callbackQuery.ts   — callback/action dispatcher
  state.ts           — shared mutable state object
```

## State machine (multi-step flows)

User state is stored in `state.ts` (in-memory object keyed by user ID). Commands check `state[userId].step` to route input.

`clearState` middleware resets state when user sends a `/` command.

## Middleware order

1. `clearState` — resets state on new slash command
2. `admin` — blocks non-admins from admin commands (checks `ADMIN_IDS`)
3. Command handlers

## Telegram vs Max API differences

| Concern | Telegram (Telegraf) | Max (@maxhub/max-bot-api) |
|---------|--------------------|-----------------------|
| Start event | `bot.start` | `bot.on('bot_started')` |
| Callbacks | `bot.action(regex, ctx)` | `bot.on('message_callback', ctx)` |
| Message text | `bot.on('message', ctx)` | `bot.on('message_created', ctx)` |
| Contact | `bot.on('contact', ctx)` | `ctx.contactInfo` in message_created |
| Parse mode | MarkdownV2 in some places | HTML everywhere |
| Edit message | `ctx.editMessageText` | Max edit API via `lib/editMessageText` |

## Admin guard

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — check before any admin action
if (!isAdmin(ctx)) return;
```

Admin IDs come from `ADMIN_IDS` (TG) or `MAX_ADMIN_IDS` (Max), comma-separated strings parsed in `config/variables.ts`.
